PLACES=[
N('fort-morgan-gulf-shores','Fort Morgan','Gulf Shores','al','fort','standing',30.22806,-88.02306,'site','open','drive','forts','1819–1834',
  'A pentagonal brick fort with five bastions at the tip of Mobile Point, designed by Simon Bernard, a French engineer who had served Napoleon, and finished in 1834 on the site of the earlier Fort Bowyer. The state of Alabama seized it in January 1861. After the Battle of Mobile Bay on August 5, 1864 federal forces besieged it until August 22, and a fire burned the citadel inside. Concrete gun batteries were added from 1895.',
  'The brick walls and courtyard, with a museum of Civil War, World War I and World War II objects. The Encyclopedia of Alabama says the officers\' houses are staff housing and not open. Wikipedia says the citadel\'s remains were taken down in the 1880s; the Encyclopedia shows a photograph captioned as its ruins, so what is left of it was not settled.',
  '',
  'Run by the Alabama Historical Commission as a state historic site. Hours are from the Commission\'s plan-your-visit page. Admission is charged and sources give different prices ($7 to $8 for adults), so check before going.'+NOPARK,
  [W('Fort Morgan (Alabama)'),['Alabama Historical Commission: Fort Morgan, plan your visit','https://ahc.alabama.gov/FtMorganPlanYourVisit.aspx'],['Encyclopedia of Alabama: Fort Morgan Museum and State Historic Site','https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/fort-morgan-museum-and-state-historic-site/']],brick='yes',
  hours='Fort and grounds daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.; museum daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas, New Year\'s Day'),
N('fort-gaines-dauphin-island','Fort Gaines','Dauphin Island','al','fort','standing',30.24833,-88.07556,'site','open','drive','forts','1821–',
  'A masonry fort at the eastern tip of Dauphin Island, begun in 1821 and named for General Edmund Gaines. Admiral Farragut\'s fleet passed it in the Battle of Mobile Bay in August 1864. It was partly modernised for the Spanish–American War. Wikipedia calls it one of the best-preserved Civil War era masonry forts in the country; erosion of the shore beside it is a continuing threat, and it was named one of America\'s eleven most endangered historic places in 2011.',
  'Five brick buildings from before the Civil War in the courtyard, a working blacksmith shop and kitchens, tunnels to the corner bastions, original cannon, the anchor of the USS Hartford and a museum (Wikipedia). Guided tours and living-history events are held.',
  '',
  'The visit page is on the Town of Dauphin Island\'s website; admission is $10 for ages 13 and over and $5 for ages 5 to 12. The parking lot on Albatross Street is mapped in OpenStreetMap and the federal map puts the land under it inside the fort\'s site; not checked on the ground.',
  [W('Fort Gaines (Alabama)'),['Town of Dauphin Island: Fort Gaines','https://www.townofdauphinisland.org/fort-gaines']],brick='some',
  th=[30.24885,-88.07618],thn='Albatross Street lot mapped in OpenStreetMap, by the fort',
  hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas Eve, Christmas Day, New Year\'s Day'),
N('huntsville-depot','Huntsville Depot','Huntsville','al','rail','standing',34.73444,-86.59083,'exact','view','walk','stations','1860',
  'Finished in 1860 as the eastern division headquarters of the Memphis and Charleston Railroad, this is the oldest surviving railroad depot in Alabama and one of the oldest in the country. Union forces took Huntsville in 1862 and used the depot as a prison for Confederate soldiers. The last regular passenger train, the Southern Railway\'s Tennessean, ran on March 30, 1968. It later served as a railroad museum.',
  'Wikipedia says graffiti left by the Confederate prisoners can still be seen on the walls, and a 1904 Porter steam locomotive stands outside. Interior not checked: in October 2024 the museum that ran the depot said it was no longer involved and the building went back to the City of Huntsville, which was considering options, so whether it can be entered now is not known.',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of Huntsville since October 2024 (Wikipedia, citing a news report). No hours found; look from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Huntsville Depot')],brick='?'),
N('ivy-green-tuscumbia','Ivy Green (Helen Keller\'s birthplace)','Tuscumbia','al','landmark','standing',34.73944,-87.70667,'exact','open','drive','','1820',
  'The main house was built in 1820 by David Keller, Helen Keller\'s grandfather, and Helen Keller was born here in 1880. A small cottage that began as a plantation office is the birthplace room. Between the two stand the well and pump where Keller first grasped communication with her teacher Anne Sullivan. A National Historic Landmark, it was made a permanent shrine in 1954, and a fire in 1972 caused minor damage.',
  'The main house, the birthplace cottage, the well and pump. Furnishings and the collection inside were not checked.',
  '',
  'Run as a museum by the Helen Keller Property Board; the hours below are from its admission page. Ticket prices not recorded. Group visits are asked to book 24 hours ahead. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Ivy Green'),['Helen Keller Birthplace: hours and admission','https://helenkellerbirthplace.org/admission/']],brick='?',
  hours='Monday to Saturday 8:30 a.m. to 4 p.m., last tour 3:45 p.m.; closed Easter, Labor Day, Thanksgiving, Dec 24 to 26, New Year\'s Day'),
N('the-oaks-tuskegee','The Oaks, Booker T. Washington\'s home','Tuskegee','al','landmark','standing',32.4282,-85.70578,'exact','view','drive','','1899',
  'The two-story Victorian house was finished in 1899 as the home of Booker T. Washington, first principal of Tuskegee Institute. The National Park Service says Tuskegee students built it with bricks the Institute made, and that it served as the family home and a place to receive prominent guests.',
  'The house is closed to visitors while the National Park Service rehabilitates it (roof, windows, beams and the front balcony and porch); its page offers a virtual tour. When open, a ranger led a 30-minute tour of two floors of original furnishings and family objects. Look from the public road and check the park\'s page for a reopening.',
  '',
  'Part of Tuskegee Institute National Historic Site, run by the National Park Service. Closed for rehabilitation as of the page read in November 2026; the park\'s hours page also lists tour times that applied before the closure. A lot beside West Montgomery Road is mapped in OpenStreetMap and lies inside the site on the federal map; not checked on the ground.',
  [['National Park Service: The Oaks','https://www.nps.gov/tuin/planyourvisit/the-oaks-home-of-booker-t-washington.htm'],['National Park Service: Tuskegee hours','https://www.nps.gov/tuin/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],brick='yes',
  th=[32.42755,-85.70577],thn='Lot on West Montgomery Road mapped in OpenStreetMap, inside the National Historic Site'),
N('16th-street-baptist-birmingham','16th Street Baptist Church','Birmingham','al','worship','standing',33.51678,-86.81498,'exact','open','walk','','1911',
  'Organised in 1873 as the First Colored Baptist Church of Birmingham, the congregation moved to this corner in 1880 and built a brick church in 1884. The present building, in what Wikipedia calls a modified Romanesque and Byzantine design by the Black architect Wallace Rayfield, dates from 1911. A bomb exploded here on September 15, 1963, killing four girls. It is a National Historic Landmark (2006) and still holds services.',
  'The church of 1911, restored in the 2000s after water and brick-facing damage. The church runs guided tours that last at least an hour. Original woodwork and glass were not checked.',
  '',
  'A working church; tours are booked through the church (205-251-9402) and days vary with church events. Hours are from the church\'s tours page. Parking not checked; the streets round it are in the Fourth Avenue Historic District.',
  [W('16th Street Baptist Church'),['16th Street Baptist Church: tours','https://16thstreetbaptist.org/tours/']],brick='?',
  hours='Tours Tuesday to Saturday, first at 10 a.m., last at 3 p.m.; book ahead, days vary with church events'),
N('oakleigh-mobile','Oakleigh Historic Complex','Mobile','al','landmark','standing',30.68086,-88.06047,'exact','open','drive','','1833–1838',
  'James Roper, a brick mason and dry-goods merchant from Virginia, began this house in 1833 and finished it in 1838. It is a Greek Revival raised villa, a frame house with its main floor lifted over a basement in the Low Country way, and one of the largest T-shaped houses in Alabama. It was documented in 1935 and opened as a museum on January 9, 1965.',
  'The house and its period furnishings, with the Cox-Deasy Cottage, the Union Barracks and an archive in the complex (Wikipedia). Wikipedia says the basement is likely handmade brick from the site. Tours are guided.',
  '',
  'Run by the Historic Mobile Preservation Society. Its own site would not open, so the hours are from the Encyclopedia of Alabama, which gives Friday, Saturday and Monday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. and Sunday 1 to 4 p.m.; a travel listing gives Tuesday to Saturday instead, so call (251-432-1281) first. Admission was given as $10 adults and $5 children. Parking: a lot on Roper Street is mapped in OpenStreetMap; not checked.',
  [['Encyclopedia of Alabama: Historic Oakleigh','https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/historic-oakleigh/'],W('Oakleigh Historic Complex (Mobile, Alabama)')],conf='mixed',brick='some',
  hours='Friday, Saturday, Monday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday 1 to 4 p.m. (one source); other listings differ, call ahead'),
N('bragg-mitchell-mansion-mobile','Bragg–Mitchell Mansion','Mobile','al','landmark','standing',30.69243,-88.0862,'site','open','drive','','1855',
  'A Greek Revival mansion of about 13,000 square feet on nearly 12 acres, it stood three miles outside the city when it was built. The Encyclopedia of Alabama says the date is unclear: some sources give 1847 and most give 1855, when Judge John Bragg bought the land. The National Register and the 1935 survey say another man built it with Thomas James as architect, while a different account has John Bragg building it with his brother Alexander as architect. It was listed in 1972 and opened to the public in the late 1980s.',
  'The house with furnishings the Braggs bought in New York and Baltimore, among them five large gilded mirrors (Encyclopedia of Alabama). Guided tours only.',
  '',
  'Open for guided tours; the house\'s own page says to call ahead because it closes for private events. Hours are from that page; closed Saturday to Monday. Street parking on Spring Hill Avenue; not checked.',
  [['Bragg-Mitchell Mansion: visitor information','https://braggmitchellmansion.com/?p=6'],['Encyclopedia of Alabama: Bragg-Mitchell Mansion','https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/bragg-mitchell-mansion/'],W('Bragg–Mitchell Mansion')],conf='mixed',brick='?',
  hours='Tuesday to Friday, tours on the hour from 10 a.m., last tour 3 p.m.; closed Saturday to Monday and holidays'),
N('first-white-house-confederacy-montgomery','First White House of the Confederacy','Montgomery','al','landmark','standing',32.37611,-86.3,'exact','open','walk','','1832–1835',
  'Built between 1832 and 1835 by the lawyer William Sayre in the Federal style, later given an Italianate look by Edmond Harrison, who leased it to the Confederate government. Jefferson Davis and his family lived here from February to May 1861, while Montgomery was the capital. It was moved in 1921 from the corner of Bibb and Lee Streets to Washington Avenue to save it when the neighbourhood was rebuilt.',
  'Wikipedia says it has a bracketed cornice round the hipped roof, sits on brick piers, and is furnished with period pieces of the 1850s and 1860s. It is open to the public. Note that the house stands on a moved site, not where Davis lived.',
  '',
  'A house museum kept by the White House Association of Alabama (Encyclopedia of Alabama). Hours differ between listings and no page of its own was read, so none are recorded; phone (334) 242-1861. Parking not checked.',
  [W('First White House of the Confederacy'),['Encyclopedia of Alabama: First White House of the Confederacy','https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/first-white-house-of-the-confederacy/']],brick='some',
  hours='?'),
N('gorgas-house-tuscaloosa','Gorgas House','Tuscaloosa','al','landmark','standing',33.21261,-87.54767,'exact','open','walk','','1829',
  'Designed by William Nichols and completed in 1829, before the University of Alabama opened, in Greek Revival style. It is one of only seven buildings that survived the burning of the campus by Union forces in 1865, and Visit Tuscaloosa calls it the oldest building on the campus. It is now a museum.',
  'The house museum on the University of Alabama campus; the nearby Little Round House, a crenellated guardhouse of 1860, also survived the burning (Wikipedia). Interior contents not checked.',
  '',
  'Run by the University of Alabama Museums. Closed on university holidays, including November 26, 2026, and from December 22, 2026 to January 5, 2027. Admission $2, free for current university staff and students. Campus parking rules were not checked.',
  [['Gorgas House Museum: visit','https://gorgashouse.museums.ua.edu/visit/'],W('Gorgas–Manly Historic District')],brick='yes',
  hours='Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to noon and 1 to 4:30 p.m.; closed university holidays'),
N('rickwood-field-birmingham','Rickwood Field','Birmingham','al','amuse','standing',33.50222,-86.85583,'exact','open','walk','','1910',
  'The oldest existing professional baseball stadium in the United States, built for the Birmingham Barons in 1910 by the industrialist and team owner Rick Woodward and finished that summer. The grandstand was roofed in 1924–27 and a Mission style entrance with offices was added in 1928. The Barons left in 1988; since 1992 the Friends of Rickwood have looked after it, and the 2024 renovation cost about $5 million.',
  'The 1910 ballpark, kept as a working museum. A self-guided tour brochure and guided tours by arrangement are offered.',
  '',
  'Cared for by the Friends of Rickwood on the City of Birmingham\'s ground. The visit page lists Saturday and Sunday as open on game days only, and a listing of the contact page gives weekday hours of 8:30 a.m. to 4 p.m. Closed on holidays. Admission was reported free. A lot on 12th Street West is mapped in OpenStreetMap and lies on the park\'s land.',
  [W('Rickwood Field'),['Rickwood Field: visit us','https://rickwood.com/visit-us/']],brick='?',
  th=[33.50256,-86.85671],thn='Lot on 12th Street West, on the park\'s land',
  hours='Monday to Friday about 8:30 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Saturday and Sunday on game days only; closed holidays'),
]

